Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-8655, "ACL: Named Hardware on port - copy-to-cpu IP", baseline plus
          3 steps
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (netboots), Marvell 98CX8514 Falcon
          swi_b  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (AT-x960-28YSQ, console u3)
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup)

VERDICT: PASS -- a named hardware ACL with the copy-to-cpu action, bound to a port,
         copies matching frames to the CPU AND still forwards them.
      copied to the CPU ............... yes        (queue 0: 0 -> 8, = its 8 ACL hits)
      still forwarded to the far side . yes        (0% packet loss on the same 8 pings)
      rule matched the intended flow .. yes        (show access-list counters: 8 hits)
      spanning tree across the step ... unchanged  (root, root port, forwarding states)
      distinct from send-to-cpu ....... yes        (same hits, opposite forwarding
                                                    outcome, different CPU queue)
CAVEAT:  The CPU half is proven by the Marvell SDMA receive-queue counter and the ACL
         hit counter agreeing exactly, not by inspecting the frames the CPU received.
         Receive-queue 6 could not be used as evidence on this bench (see the method
         note in STEP 1), so this run says nothing about which queue, other than 0,
         the action may also touch. The traffic was ICMP echo matched by an `ip` rule
         from one source to one destination; no other protocol or address form was
         tried, and only the ingress direction on port1.0.48 was exercised.

