STEP 0 -- baseline: spanning tree before any CPU-action ACL               done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Captured because a CPU-bound ACL action can disturb control traffic, and a
  documented CR (CR30477) describes spanning-tree interference from it. RSTP is
  running on all three units.
x960-56YSQ#show spanning-tree brief
  Default: Root Id 8000:0000cd423029
  Default: Bridge Id 8000:e01aead472cb
  sa1             8000:0000cd423029    8195       Rootport         Forwarding
  port1.0.10      8000:e01aead472cb    8392       Designated       Forwarding
  port1.0.48      8000:e01aead472cb    83b8       Designated       Forwarding

STEP 1 -- "apply copy-to-cpu on a port and confirm the action"            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: copy-to-cpu is defined as "copy to the CPU AND forward". Proving only the
  CPU half would not distinguish it from send-to-cpu; proving only the forwarding half
  would not distinguish it from no rule at all. Both halves are measured here.

  Method note on the counters: `show platform counter sdma` exposes the Marvell SDMA
  receive-queue counters. rxDmaPcktCnt[0] is a plain cumulative counter, it sits at 0
  on this bench and moves only when a copy-to-cpu rule matches, so it is clean
  evidence. rxDmaPcktCnt[6] is CLEAR-ON-READ and carries this bench's continuous
  background control traffic (RSTP BPDUs, LLDP, and loop-protection detect frames at
  one per second on every port); its measured idle rate was 40 counts in 8 s, against
  which a ten-packet probe was not separable (the ACL measurements below used eight). Queue 6 is therefore NOT used as evidence,
  and its readings must not be read as a negative result.

x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44991
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#copy-to-cpu ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group t44991
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44991
      4 copy-to-cpu ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32

  HALF ONE -- copied to the CPU:
x960-56YSQ#show platform counter sdma        (before, then after 8 pings)
              0 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]   ->      8 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters t44991
      Packet Hits: 8
  CPU receive-queue 0 advanced from 0 to 8, EXACTLY matching the 8 ACL hits.

  HALF TWO -- still forwarded:
host# ping -c 8 -i 0.3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  0% packet loss                            <-- the far side still received and replied
  => STEP 1 PASS: matching frames were copied to the CPU and were ALSO still forwarded
     to the far side. The exact agreement between the CPU queue delta and the ACL hit
     count is what makes the CPU half positive proof rather than coincidence, and the
     0% loss is what distinguishes copy-to-cpu from send-to-cpu.

STEP 2 -- control traffic undisturbed by the CPU-bound action             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ#show spanning-tree brief
  Default: Root Id 8000:0000cd423029        <-- unchanged
  sa1             8000:0000cd423029    8195       Rootport         Forwarding
  port1.0.48      8000:e01aead472cb    83b8       Designated       Forwarding
  => STEP 2 PASS: the root bridge, the root port and every forwarding port are
     unchanged from the STEP 0 baseline across the copy-to-cpu step. No spanning-tree
     disturbance on this build.

STEP 3 -- copy-to-cpu against send-to-cpu: the contrast that gives this
           action its meaning                                             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the send-to-cpu action was run back to back with this one on the identical
  setup, binding point and traffic, so the two readings differ only in the action.

  action        ACL hits   CPU queue 0    forwarded to the far side?
  copy-to-cpu       8        0 -> 8       YES  (0% packet loss)
  send-to-cpu       8        0 -> 0       NO   (100% packet loss)

  Both rules matched the same 8 packets, so the difference is entirely in what the
  switch did with them. copy-to-cpu also uses a different CPU path: queue 0 moves for
  copy-to-cpu and does not move at all for send-to-cpu.
  => STEP 3 PASS: the pair behaves exactly to the documented definitions and each is
     the other's control. Neither reading could have been produced by the other action,
     so this run identifies copy-to-cpu positively rather than by absence of an error.
